q("Relational Design", "Easy", "What is a natural key?", ["A real-world attribute or combination that naturally identifies a row", "A randomly generated number only", "A deleted foreign key", "A query alias"], 0, "A natural key derives from meaningful domain data, such as an approved account number.")
q("Relational Design", "Medium", "What can a nullable foreign key represent?", ["An optional relationship", "A required relationship in every case", "A duplicate primary key", "A table with no schema"], 0, "NULL in a nullable foreign key can indicate that a related entity is not applicable or not yet assigned.")
q("Relational Design", "Easy", "What does relationship cardinality describe?", ["How many instances of one entity can relate to another", "The number of database users", "The physical disk size", "The SQL keyword count"], 0, "Cardinality describes relationship counts such as one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many.")
q("Relational Design", "Easy", "In relational design, what commonly represents an entity attribute?", ["A column", "A database server", "A transaction log", "A query plan"], 0, "An entity attribute is usually stored as a column in the relation representing that entity.")
q("Relational Design", "Medium", "What is a common primary key for a junction table?", ["The combination of its two foreign keys", "A random column that is always NULL", "The table name", "A trigger name"], 0, "A composite key of the participating foreign keys can prevent duplicate relationships.")
q("Relational Design", "Easy", "What do created_at and updated_at columns commonly support?", ["Basic record audit and lifecycle tracking", "Foreign-key encryption", "Automatic normalization", "Query parsing"], 0, "Timestamp columns can record when a row was created and last changed, supporting audit and operational needs.")

